1
00:00:03,064 --> 00:00:14,867
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in relation to what we've been saying in the last few talks regarding the mind, wandering or the similes, what would the gratification of sensuality be?

2
00:00:20,909 --> 00:00:23,089
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you have internal resistance without sensuality?

3
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:28,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

4
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:30,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Today is not quite the beginning of sensuality.

5
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,401
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's the proximate cause, more often than not.

6
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:39,282
[SPEAKER_03]: When people want to avoid pain, they tend to sensuality.

7
00:00:39,882 --> 00:00:43,823
[SPEAKER_03]: But just within sensuality, where is its origin?

8
00:00:44,123 --> 00:00:47,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is that gratification?

9
00:00:53,146 --> 00:00:54,086
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the gratification?

10
00:00:54,106 --> 00:00:55,046
[SPEAKER_03]: You still haven't answered that.

11
00:00:55,987 --> 00:00:56,387
[SPEAKER_03]: Delight.

12
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:02,774
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the zeal, the delight, towards it.

13
00:01:02,794 --> 00:01:03,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards the pleasure.

14
00:01:03,895 --> 00:01:04,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards the pleasure, yeah.

15
00:01:05,215 --> 00:01:06,475
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards the prospect of pleasure.

16
00:01:07,176 --> 00:01:15,318
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that, it's like, the thing that makes it pleasant is the delight, not the kind of idea of pleasure.

17
00:01:15,558 --> 00:01:16,359
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

18
00:01:16,379 --> 00:01:18,719
[SPEAKER_03]: The delight is already its gratification.

19
00:01:18,839 --> 00:01:19,480
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

20
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:22,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you asking in terms of the simile, in terms of the animal?

21
00:01:22,721 --> 00:01:23,701
[SPEAKER_03]: In terms of the whole thing.

22
00:01:24,341 --> 00:01:24,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Everything.

23
00:01:24,881 --> 00:01:26,922
[SPEAKER_03]: The mind, you, whatever you may be saying.

24
00:01:29,143 --> 00:01:35,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind can see only you, precious you, towards things you want, through you.

25
00:01:37,207 --> 00:01:38,647
[SPEAKER_03]: You are its puppet, basically.

26
00:01:40,328 --> 00:01:44,730
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, the five strings, they're called strings because they control you like a puppet.

27
00:01:45,751 --> 00:01:49,232
[SPEAKER_03]: Like a puppeteer controls a doll.

28
00:01:50,413 --> 00:01:51,133
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh...

29
00:01:54,773 --> 00:02:09,327
[SPEAKER_03]: And yes, you know, you're acting toward pleasure, because you want to avoid pain, you want to avoid pressure, you want to avoid resistance, but all of that's kind of secondary to the origin of that gratification, that delight.

30
00:02:17,835 --> 00:02:18,816
[SPEAKER_02]: So, where's that delight?

31
00:02:22,408 --> 00:02:33,759
[SPEAKER_03]: But I'm just asking, so, from the point of view of, if you imagine or recollect a sensual desire of any kind, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, there is pressure, there is craving.

32
00:02:36,141 --> 00:02:45,049
[SPEAKER_03]: Even before you engage with the object, the gratification is there through the presence of delight.

33
00:02:45,189 --> 00:02:46,751
[SPEAKER_03]: Where does that delight come from?

34
00:02:50,771 --> 00:02:51,331
Come, mind.

35
00:02:52,012 --> 00:02:52,432
[SPEAKER_03]: Which one?

36
00:02:52,952 --> 00:02:53,413
[SPEAKER_03]: Which mind?

37
00:02:56,775 --> 00:02:58,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Your mind or the animal mind?

38
00:02:58,936 --> 00:02:59,556
[SPEAKER_03]: The animal's mind.

39
00:02:59,576 --> 00:03:00,037
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal.

40
00:03:00,077 --> 00:03:07,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where... it's like a... untamed... in... kind of...

41
00:03:07,761 --> 00:03:11,263
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the joy towards its reality comes from the animal.

42
00:03:11,623 --> 00:03:12,284
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what you're saying?

43
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:12,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

44
00:03:13,725 --> 00:03:14,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody else?

45
00:03:19,799 --> 00:03:22,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I would have said, you know.

46
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's correct.

47
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:24,261
[SPEAKER_03]: But I want to check.

48
00:03:26,942 --> 00:03:27,682
[SPEAKER_03]: It does, it does.

49
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:28,723
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the whole point.

50
00:03:29,523 --> 00:03:31,744
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you could be telling yourself all you want.

51
00:03:33,144 --> 00:03:36,266
[SPEAKER_03]: That centrality of as little satisfaction and all this.

52
00:03:37,146 --> 00:03:42,368
[SPEAKER_03]: When the mind wants it, you see no escape from that.

53
00:03:43,109 --> 00:03:43,849
[SPEAKER_03]: You are under that.

54
00:03:43,869 --> 00:03:46,230
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why the only way to begin that sort of

55
00:03:48,488 --> 00:03:51,130
[SPEAKER_03]: To begin liberating yourself from it is through sense restraint first.

56
00:03:51,230 --> 00:03:55,332
[SPEAKER_03]: Simply to not give in towards what citta wants you to do.

57
00:03:56,033 --> 00:03:58,114
[SPEAKER_03]: Wants to use you as a puppet, basically.

58
00:03:58,835 --> 00:04:02,837
[SPEAKER_03]: To... to experience things through you.

59
00:04:03,257 --> 00:04:05,599
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it only can see... it can only see you.

60
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:17,787
[SPEAKER_03]: And... and that's... so for as long as you don't, basically, if you would not have discerned the mind for what it is, you can't even see the origin.

61
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:25,185
[SPEAKER_03]: So, your efforts towards sensuality would not be uprooting it.

62
00:04:27,306 --> 00:04:33,572
[SPEAKER_03]: It would not be addressing the problem for what it is, which is... No, no, no.

63
00:04:33,632 --> 00:04:40,958
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to convey to the mind that it's actually better to not want it for itself, not for you.

64
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:56,606
[SPEAKER_03]: And because it is factually better to not want it, it is a burning ember even for the wild animal, it can then get, you know, it can get that message, if you're able to communicate to it.

65
00:04:58,907 --> 00:05:01,228
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's then when you go beyond sensuality, i.e.

66
00:05:01,328 --> 00:05:05,991
[SPEAKER_03]: when your own animal is not pressuring you towards it anymore, not when you chose to do so or something.

67
00:05:07,492 --> 00:05:10,253
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why the Buddha would say in the suttas, like, even he would

68
00:05:11,415 --> 00:05:13,256
[SPEAKER_03]: In the beginning, when he would be like...

69
00:05:13,776 --> 00:05:21,521
[SPEAKER_03]: I saw the extent how unsatisfactory sensuality is, how little joy it provides, how more peril...

70
00:05:21,841 --> 00:05:24,803
[SPEAKER_03]: Yet, still, my mind would not leap towards that.

71
00:05:25,523 --> 00:05:26,504
[SPEAKER_03]: It would still not want it.

72
00:05:27,604 --> 00:05:28,185
[SPEAKER_03]: And then I thought...

73
00:05:28,205 --> 00:05:28,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Renunciation.

74
00:05:29,565 --> 00:05:30,886
[SPEAKER_03]: Renunciation, yeah.

75
00:05:30,986 --> 00:05:32,667
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like, oh, why is that the case?

76
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:35,129
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, because he hasn't really...

77
00:05:35,149 --> 00:05:38,771
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he hasn't communicated the danger of it to the mind well enough.

78
00:05:39,734 --> 00:05:44,098
[SPEAKER_03]: And when it did, then the mind started preferring as well, not going into sincerity.

79
00:05:49,402 --> 00:05:53,906
[SPEAKER_03]: But people often think, well, normally, obviously, what else can they think?

80
00:05:54,527 --> 00:06:03,394
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you don't start by seeing the mind, you don't start by discerning that space in between as a phenomenon, real as such, peripherally known.

81
00:06:03,835 --> 00:06:05,857
[SPEAKER_03]: Then you just think, okay, I have this desire,

82
00:06:06,610 --> 00:06:24,083
[SPEAKER_03]: But at the same time, I don't want it, I see how bad it is, but I can't help it, but then I do it, then I can't stop doing it, then I stop doing it, then I start doing it again, and it's just this constant sort of turmoil and internal conflict of, well, seemingly wanting it and not wanting it at the same time.

83
00:06:26,325 --> 00:06:31,689
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's... you could well not be wanting it through and through, as far as you're concerned.

84
00:06:32,547 --> 00:06:34,969
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not where the power comes from.

85
00:06:34,989 --> 00:06:39,573
[SPEAKER_03]: It comes from the fact that the wild animal, that is still invisible to you, wants it.

86
00:06:40,774 --> 00:06:41,855
[SPEAKER_03]: And you don't even see the animal.

87
00:06:41,875 --> 00:06:42,976
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can you then talk to it?

88
00:06:43,776 --> 00:06:47,579
[SPEAKER_03]: How can you then get the timing right to convey the message and so on?

89
00:06:53,364 --> 00:06:56,647
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you then convey to your mind that the renunciation is good for it?

90
00:07:02,233 --> 00:07:04,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to start by, kind of, renouncing physically.

91
00:07:06,055 --> 00:07:07,036
[SPEAKER_03]: 100%.

92
00:07:07,056 --> 00:07:08,617
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you won't even see your mind if you don't.

93
00:07:08,897 --> 00:07:10,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I think we said that.

94
00:07:10,558 --> 00:07:12,039
[SPEAKER_03]: We must have said that in the previous talks.

95
00:07:12,159 --> 00:07:17,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you don't see your mind by somehow directing your attention to the neither here nor there in between.

96
00:07:18,543 --> 00:07:23,747
[SPEAKER_03]: You see it by withdrawing the threshold of your perception from the sense objects.

97
00:07:23,987 --> 00:07:25,308
[SPEAKER_03]: Eye through sensory strain.

98
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:32,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Through not fueling desire and lust for those objects anymore, you get to then see the internal counterparts and so on.

99
00:07:33,222 --> 00:07:34,403
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because you chose to.

100
00:07:38,544 --> 00:07:43,466
[SPEAKER_03]: So, say somebody is obviously seeing the mind, which means they would have been established in virtue.

101
00:07:44,706 --> 00:07:49,048
[SPEAKER_03]: They're not approaching it through some sort of sensation watching in the area of their heart.

102
00:07:50,644 --> 00:07:53,107
[SPEAKER_03]: Which, obviously, won't be the mind.

103
00:07:53,488 --> 00:08:05,885
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how would they then start conveying the message that renunciation is what the mind also actually wants, even when it goes towards sensuality?

104
00:08:12,777 --> 00:08:26,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we went through the, like, list, last talk, which one, of, like, yeah, physical, verbal, and then, yeah, yeah, sense restraints, and then, okay, thoughts, the images, like...

105
00:08:26,469 --> 00:08:35,097
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you also, now, like, in in in this, in this example of, specifically, when it comes to sensuality, you want to

106
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:42,538
[SPEAKER_03]: Present it with certain contexts, because the mind needs to see it for itself.

107
00:08:42,979 --> 00:08:46,481
[SPEAKER_03]: The dog needs to get the message, not just emulate your behavior.

108
00:08:47,842 --> 00:08:54,347
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, without emulating your behavior, there'll be no message, but what's the message?

109
00:08:54,387 --> 00:08:55,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Correct contemplation.

110
00:08:55,888 --> 00:09:06,256
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, the mind sees you, so you can choose what you choose to, like, how you choose to attend to things.

111
00:09:08,512 --> 00:09:12,015
[SPEAKER_00]: The way you attend to things will kind of...

112
00:09:12,055 --> 00:09:16,358
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the way you attend, yeah, but does it also matter what you attend?

113
00:09:16,418 --> 00:09:17,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, that's what I mean.

114
00:09:17,619 --> 00:09:29,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the way you attend, they kind of go together in this particular instance, because obviously you can't do, like, the correct contemplation of the danger of sensuality.

115
00:09:29,977 --> 00:09:33,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having a certain way of attending to that danger as well.

116
00:09:34,319 --> 00:09:35,280
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

117
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:38,261
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's that's what the suttas would say as well.

118
00:09:38,981 --> 00:09:43,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, what everyone frequently thinks and ponders, that's what his mind starts to incline towards.

119
00:09:45,064 --> 00:09:50,387
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you realize, oh, yeah, so the dog hates renunciation, doesn't want to hear about it, but obviously, I'm the one

120
00:09:51,303 --> 00:09:51,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Change it.

121
00:09:52,403 --> 00:09:54,124
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm the one responsible for training it.

122
00:09:54,224 --> 00:09:56,205
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'll just keep presenting this context to it.

123
00:09:56,685 --> 00:09:58,886
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's now that part of how you attend to it.

124
00:09:59,427 --> 00:10:07,250
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you could just go into like mindless body contemplation as the traditional way of body parts would think it's done.

125
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:08,771
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, no, no.

126
00:10:08,791 --> 00:10:13,073
[SPEAKER_03]: You gotta actually present it with the factual danger that would be felt.

127
00:10:14,378 --> 00:10:21,421
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it needs to be like a living contemplation, not just going through the mental motions of images of body parts and stuff.

128
00:10:21,461 --> 00:10:22,742
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

129
00:10:22,762 --> 00:10:25,543
[SPEAKER_03]: There is an actual danger in this.

130
00:10:25,683 --> 00:10:26,944
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to feel the danger.

131
00:10:27,004 --> 00:10:28,325
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to recognize the bait.

132
00:10:29,285 --> 00:10:32,127
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, there is poison in that thing that you want to eat right there.

133
00:10:33,027 --> 00:10:33,867
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, thank you.

134
00:10:33,887 --> 00:10:34,728
[SPEAKER_03]: That was so close.

135
00:10:34,848 --> 00:10:37,089
[SPEAKER_03]: If you didn't tell me, I would have consumed it.

136
00:10:38,382 --> 00:10:41,124
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the only way for the mind to get the message across.

137
00:10:41,804 --> 00:10:47,228
[SPEAKER_03]: No amount of just, you know, teacher, student, government, curriculum, information, past...

138
00:10:48,388 --> 00:10:56,954
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, this sutta says that, and that sutta says this, and there is 32 parts, and there is 16 ways of going about it, and there you go, you have finished your contemplation of the Asubha.

139
00:10:58,415 --> 00:11:00,436
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, no, no, the mind has to feel that.

140
00:11:01,917 --> 00:11:06,080
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that's... you keep doing that for as long as the mind still inclines towards it.

141
00:11:07,574 --> 00:11:17,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Until, actually, you realize... and you would know, because the mind... well, if the mind still inclines towards it, or if not, that becomes your feedback.

142
00:11:17,844 --> 00:11:19,125
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly, that's it.

143
00:11:19,145 --> 00:11:23,129
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, oh, look, I can stop now, because it's not actually trying to go to that.

144
00:11:23,709 --> 00:11:25,651
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't want it anymore.

145
00:11:25,711 --> 00:11:28,914
[SPEAKER_03]: It genuinely wants to have nothing to do with it.

146
00:11:32,408 --> 00:11:33,808
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you protect it in that state.

147
00:11:33,828 --> 00:11:37,469
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't get careless and start presenting it.

148
00:11:37,509 --> 00:11:50,531
[SPEAKER_03]: Because each time you choose to look, smell, taste, touch, hear, unsuitable sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, it's unsuitable for the mind.

149
00:11:51,371 --> 00:11:53,532
[SPEAKER_03]: As I said before, you can well handle it.

150
00:11:54,032 --> 00:11:55,192
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't do anything to me.

151
00:11:56,272 --> 00:11:59,333
[SPEAKER_03]: But is that animal fully, completely, utterly, 100% trained

152
00:12:00,768 --> 00:12:02,610
[SPEAKER_03]: Without any underlying tendencies left in it?

153
00:12:04,072 --> 00:12:04,652
[SPEAKER_03]: Or it's not?

154
00:12:04,832 --> 00:12:05,994
[SPEAKER_03]: And you know the answer already.

155
00:12:06,735 --> 00:12:09,978
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if it's not, these things are unsuitable for it.

156
00:12:10,539 --> 00:12:11,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Although, you're fine.

157
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:12,321
[SPEAKER_03]: Nobody denies that.

158
00:12:12,701 --> 00:12:14,623
[SPEAKER_03]: You're the greatest, the most restrained person.

159
00:12:14,643 --> 00:12:16,205
[SPEAKER_03]: You can hold everything in the world.

160
00:12:16,986 --> 00:12:18,667
[SPEAKER_03]: But the mind that's not developed can't.

161
00:12:19,468 --> 00:12:22,432
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in the same sense, you would be presenting it with the right contexts.

162
00:12:23,043 --> 00:12:23,804
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I mean, yeah.

163
00:12:41,972 --> 00:12:45,913
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, look, this good thing again, that I always wanted.

164
00:12:46,974 --> 00:12:49,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've always been training it for something.

165
00:12:49,634 --> 00:12:50,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, exactly.

166
00:12:50,955 --> 00:12:51,975
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no neutral.

167
00:12:52,075 --> 00:12:54,396
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's either suitable or unsuitable.

168
00:12:54,556 --> 00:12:56,036
[SPEAKER_03]: Beneficial or unbeneficial.

169
00:12:57,337 --> 00:13:03,118
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's recognized that... because sometimes people is like... and that's exactly how people would like go against the precepts or census change.

170
00:13:03,178 --> 00:13:06,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I can... it's not a problem for me.

171
00:13:07,340 --> 00:13:08,200
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's factually true.

172
00:13:09,548 --> 00:13:12,149
[SPEAKER_03]: Probably more often than not, it's like, oh, it's not a problem for me.

173
00:13:13,369 --> 00:13:15,589
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's like, oh, yeah, but the mind will get the wrong message.

174
00:13:16,490 --> 00:13:28,313
[SPEAKER_03]: If I look at these things, taste these things, and when I say unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, it means done through that intention of distraction, sensuality.

175
00:13:28,553 --> 00:13:29,913
[SPEAKER_03]: You know what type they are.

176
00:13:30,913 --> 00:13:35,314
[SPEAKER_03]: And that will automatically be sending a message to the mind.

177
00:13:35,714 --> 00:13:38,175
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if you say, oh, do it quietly, so you won't see it.

178
00:13:39,592 --> 00:13:40,714
[SPEAKER_03]: It is you doing it.

179
00:13:40,734 --> 00:13:43,718
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be doing it if it weren't for the... Well, exactly, exactly.

180
00:13:43,818 --> 00:13:48,525
[SPEAKER_03]: Once you don't do it, then you realize, oh, the mind is basically already... there was an underlying pressure from the mind.

181
00:13:48,585 --> 00:13:49,666
[SPEAKER_03]: Come on, come on, just this once.

182
00:13:53,091 --> 00:13:55,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... you can...

183
00:13:56,908 --> 00:13:59,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You're aware of the mind leaning in a certain direction.

184
00:13:59,349 --> 00:14:03,931
[SPEAKER_01]: It wants to, okay, the eye, you know, it wants to look somewhere.

185
00:14:05,252 --> 00:14:06,252
[SPEAKER_01]: It wants to tend something.

186
00:14:06,372 --> 00:14:09,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know, you can feel that, that it wants to do that.

187
00:14:11,594 --> 00:14:21,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And so you start noticing the ugly aspects of the situation while it's trying to look at the beautiful.

188
00:14:22,954 --> 00:14:23,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

189
00:14:23,474 --> 00:14:26,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see the animal first, in order to present the context.

190
00:14:26,657 --> 00:14:27,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

191
00:14:27,738 --> 00:14:31,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you see it leaning in a specific direction, that's what I mean.

192
00:14:32,222 --> 00:14:33,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, right, right.

193
00:14:33,123 --> 00:14:41,971
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, when you're thinking of the Asuba context, you're not thinking it to cancel your own thoughts, you're thinking it just to broaden the context for the animal.

194
00:14:42,011 --> 00:14:49,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I don't know if it's kind of obvious where it's leaning, to get the flavor of where it's leaning, that direction, and then you bring up

195
00:14:51,510 --> 00:14:53,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever other ugly context... Sure, sure, sure.

196
00:14:54,112 --> 00:14:59,497
[SPEAKER_03]: If it's done for the mind, it's being seen peripherally, yeah.

197
00:15:00,097 --> 00:15:05,422
[SPEAKER_03]: Not if it's done just as a response to, oh, I must think the opposite.

198
00:15:09,333 --> 00:15:15,497
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm just clarifying that whoever listens to this does not jump to conclusion of, oh, so I must think the opposite every time something presents itself.

199
00:15:16,117 --> 00:15:16,738
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

200
00:15:17,278 --> 00:15:18,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm wondering if it's not a response.

201
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the mind is leaning in the direction... You could say it is a form of response.

202
00:15:23,482 --> 00:15:24,142
[SPEAKER_03]: You are responding.

203
00:15:24,162 --> 00:15:25,203
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not trying to deny that.

204
00:15:25,283 --> 00:15:25,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

205
00:15:26,163 --> 00:15:29,726
[SPEAKER_03]: But is your response rooted in trying to deny the whole thing because it's unpleasant?

206
00:15:30,506 --> 00:15:32,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Or is your response, yes, yes, there is this beautiful.

207
00:15:33,028 --> 00:15:33,788
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what you want.

208
00:15:33,968 --> 00:15:34,869
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, I understand.

209
00:15:35,249 --> 00:15:36,510
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is what comes with it.

210
00:15:37,562 --> 00:15:41,383
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you present everything else on top of that thing, not instead.

211
00:15:42,324 --> 00:15:42,544
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

212
00:15:42,804 --> 00:15:43,244
[SPEAKER_03]: Together.

213
00:15:43,904 --> 00:15:45,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's like, oh, okay.

214
00:15:45,384 --> 00:15:47,265
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like that sutta where...

215
00:15:48,145 --> 00:15:52,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, that I translated as the seeing the other side.

216
00:15:53,207 --> 00:15:54,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the bigger picture.

217
00:15:54,127 --> 00:15:54,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

218
00:15:54,547 --> 00:15:55,668
[SPEAKER_01]: The other half of the picture.

219
00:15:56,188 --> 00:16:01,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about denying the one side and then just focusing on the other.

220
00:16:01,390 --> 00:16:01,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

221
00:16:01,990 --> 00:16:03,350
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

222
00:16:03,370 --> 00:16:04,030
[SPEAKER_03]: That's absolutely fine.

223
00:16:04,050 --> 00:16:05,771
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just that, obviously, it can sound the same

224
00:16:07,157 --> 00:16:09,518
[SPEAKER_03]: to somebody who is not even seeing the mind.

225
00:16:10,559 --> 00:16:11,480
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's really the point.

226
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:16,723
[SPEAKER_03]: See, like, when you're trying to deny things, you're aiming that denial at the mind.

227
00:16:17,832 --> 00:16:19,133
[SPEAKER_03]: At the whole pressure, at everything.

228
00:16:19,153 --> 00:16:20,135
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's no communication.

229
00:16:20,155 --> 00:16:21,496
[SPEAKER_03]: There's just denial of everything.

230
00:16:21,536 --> 00:16:23,418
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and that's how people do a subha.

231
00:16:23,938 --> 00:16:25,260
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how people do loving-kindness.

232
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:27,382
[SPEAKER_03]: I just just want to replace the whole thing.

233
00:16:27,402 --> 00:16:28,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it's going to be done in the wrong time.

234
00:16:29,524 --> 00:16:30,425
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, exactly.

235
00:16:30,445 --> 00:16:31,807
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the whole thing is already wrong.

236
00:16:31,907 --> 00:16:32,187
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

237
00:16:32,507 --> 00:16:34,930
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the mind is still getting the message of aversion through it.

238
00:16:35,010 --> 00:16:36,332
[SPEAKER_03]: So, now, it's going to be even more of us.

239
00:16:36,372 --> 00:16:38,674
[SPEAKER_03]: So, next time, you're going to have even more hate.

240
00:16:40,742 --> 00:16:48,105
[SPEAKER_03]: So, or you keep presenting a subha, trying to just blindly, not teach the mind to not want it, but just blindly cancel it.

241
00:16:48,385 --> 00:16:57,568
[SPEAKER_03]: He'll learn to love that, and he'll get attracted to a subha now, because you're presenting it on top of its own existing undiminished delight and desire.

242
00:16:58,368 --> 00:17:03,030
[SPEAKER_03]: He's going to start seeing beautiful in that, as it happened so many times in the suttas.

243
00:17:06,175 --> 00:17:08,216
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's... that's already the difference between you and I.

244
00:17:08,236 --> 00:17:15,919
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you just justifying your attempts at managing the... the arisen discomforts or are you really trying to uproot them?

245
00:17:18,060 --> 00:17:27,383
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you realize, yeah, this is uncomfortable, this pressure, but it's like, okay, so I could just try and deny the whole thing and distract myself from it, basically, in the process of doing so, so I don't have to deal with it.

246
00:17:28,224 --> 00:17:31,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Or I could, like, really address the root cause.

247
00:17:32,335 --> 00:17:36,919
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is the animal delighting in things it shouldn't, and I'm being pressured on account of it.

248
00:17:37,859 --> 00:17:39,941
[SPEAKER_03]: And I'm being controlled and pushed and so on.

249
00:17:40,722 --> 00:17:41,382
[SPEAKER_03]: Like a puppet.

250
00:17:42,803 --> 00:17:47,867
[SPEAKER_03]: And for that, it takes a bit more nuance than just, okay, just think the opposite thoughts.

251
00:17:49,148 --> 00:17:55,353
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, no, you have to discern... Well, first you have to stop trying to get rid of the pressure, because then you won't discern the pressure.

252
00:17:55,593 --> 00:17:57,174
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't discern the extent of the pressure.

253
00:17:57,194 --> 00:18:00,177
[SPEAKER_03]: Because by discerning the extent of the pressure, you discern the extent of the mind.

254
00:18:02,417 --> 00:18:07,720
[SPEAKER_03]: So now, while the pressure is there, you feed the right context to it.

255
00:18:08,540 --> 00:18:09,221
[SPEAKER_03]: You present it.

256
00:18:10,341 --> 00:18:13,843
[SPEAKER_03]: And even if he doesn't want it, you keep them there, while the pressure is there.

257
00:18:14,364 --> 00:18:15,044
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the training.

258
00:18:15,064 --> 00:18:18,526
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the enduring on the right level, as he keeps saying.

259
00:18:19,907 --> 00:18:20,667
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the right level.

260
00:18:21,687 --> 00:18:24,229
[SPEAKER_03]: Pressure cannot be acted out, shouldn't be acted out of.

261
00:18:26,065 --> 00:18:29,907
[SPEAKER_03]: This is the whole picture that you're ignoring, while you're pressuring me.

262
00:18:30,527 --> 00:18:30,768
[SPEAKER_03]: Same.

263
00:18:31,248 --> 00:18:45,155
[SPEAKER_03]: Imagine a little spoiled brat, cries and screams to get what he wants, and it's causing a scene publicly, and you're feeling in the eyes of everybody, on you, because of what your unruly kid is doing.

264
00:18:46,576 --> 00:18:48,577
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, you could... so it stops.

265
00:18:50,052 --> 00:18:53,433
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, you just convey the message to that kid.

266
00:18:53,693 --> 00:18:57,215
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, next time I want something, I'll do exactly the same thing and I'll get what I want.

267
00:19:01,176 --> 00:19:07,179
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you don't give in to that pressure, it's going to feel uncomfortable, but then the kid will get the right message.

268
00:19:07,239 --> 00:19:08,619
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, this does not work.

269
00:19:10,040 --> 00:19:11,100
[SPEAKER_03]: And then it will stop trying.

270
00:19:11,401 --> 00:19:12,021
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

271
00:19:13,461 --> 00:19:14,782
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't reason with it.

272
00:19:15,870 --> 00:19:19,052
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it doesn't think like that, it doesn't think on that level.

273
00:19:19,092 --> 00:19:24,995
[SPEAKER_03]: But you could indicate the benefits and the non-benefits of that behavior.

274
00:19:25,596 --> 00:19:27,256
[SPEAKER_03]: And then it can feel that for itself.

275
00:19:35,301 --> 00:19:37,983
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you know that Talaputta, the poet,

276
00:19:41,406 --> 00:19:53,543
[SPEAKER_03]: You could read that poetically, like, you know, he's like a poet talking to his own mind, but you could also understand it phenomenologically, like he was actually addressing his mind, the phenomenon of his mind that he very much clearly seen and understood.

277
00:19:55,349 --> 00:20:10,372
[SPEAKER_03]: And he was saying like, oh, before, when he was a householder, he was romanticizing about, you know, committing his life to the practice and living in the mossy forests with deers and streams in the mountains where no people go and practice in seclusion and complete his training and so on.

278
00:20:11,212 --> 00:20:19,614
[SPEAKER_03]: But now, when he is living in the forests with moss and streams and mountains and where deer comes and so on and no people go, the mind wants to go back to the central life.

279
00:20:21,669 --> 00:20:23,690
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, no, now you're gonna stay here.

280
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:34,354
[SPEAKER_03]: You brought me here, now I'm gonna keep you here, until you get used to this and start to like it and want it, whatever it takes, how long it takes doesn't matter.

281
00:20:35,094 --> 00:20:35,615
[SPEAKER_03]: And then it did.

282
00:20:36,335 --> 00:20:37,335
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you became an arahant.

283
00:20:43,018 --> 00:20:49,060
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because the delight comes from the animal, that's why you need to teach it the danger of it.

284
00:20:51,346 --> 00:20:51,686
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not...

285
00:20:51,706 --> 00:20:53,527
[SPEAKER_03]: I already know the enjoyment to think about it.

286
00:20:54,208 --> 00:20:54,948
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not about you.

287
00:20:56,369 --> 00:21:00,672
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about getting through that... getting that message through to the... to the animal.

288
00:21:01,012 --> 00:21:05,855
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, until it actually sees, oh, yes.

289
00:21:05,895 --> 00:21:07,816
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the escape becomes obvious.

290
00:21:08,116 --> 00:21:08,977
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be the escape?

291
00:21:11,438 --> 00:21:12,059
[SPEAKER_03]: For both of you.

292
00:21:12,139 --> 00:21:13,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

293
00:21:13,720 --> 00:21:18,663
[SPEAKER_03]: Complete absence of any trace of the light coming from the animal and we're both freed.

294
00:21:25,183 --> 00:21:34,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like the animal's winding itself... wants to wind itself up, let's say, with thoughts of complaining about whatever.

295
00:21:34,169 --> 00:21:40,052
[SPEAKER_01]: It just wants to delight in the thoughts about complaining about others or whatever.

296
00:21:41,773 --> 00:21:47,177
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see that it's discerned and you go, okay, well, don't do that.

297
00:21:48,418 --> 00:21:50,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Just don't go in that direction.

298
00:21:53,857 --> 00:21:55,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we won't be going there.

299
00:21:55,738 --> 00:21:59,260
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you won't be... you don't let it out, but it wants to.

300
00:21:59,761 --> 00:22:00,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

301
00:22:01,102 --> 00:22:04,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So it calms down, because you have... you've put that boundary for it.

302
00:22:04,844 --> 00:22:05,064
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

303
00:22:05,845 --> 00:22:06,545
[SPEAKER_01]: And it calms down.

304
00:22:06,625 --> 00:22:10,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you note whatever it is after some period of time.

305
00:22:11,488 --> 00:22:11,688
[SPEAKER_01]: See?

306
00:22:12,809 --> 00:22:13,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this better?

307
00:22:13,429 --> 00:22:14,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Factually better?

308
00:22:14,410 --> 00:22:14,610
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

309
00:22:16,111 --> 00:22:16,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

310
00:22:17,092 --> 00:22:18,633
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's where the recognition would come.

311
00:22:19,457 --> 00:22:23,778
[SPEAKER_03]: See, that's like why so many times it's said in the suttas, but obviously it's so easily overread.

312
00:22:23,898 --> 00:22:26,419
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, virtue results in samādhi.

313
00:22:28,540 --> 00:22:30,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing else results in samādhi.

314
00:22:30,561 --> 00:22:35,962
[SPEAKER_03]: No, like, no concentrating and focusing could result in composing the wild animal.

315
00:22:36,583 --> 00:22:37,643
[SPEAKER_03]: Makes no difference to it.

316
00:22:38,283 --> 00:22:44,685
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, in fact, actually makes a negative difference to it, because you're focusing more on the objects of your senses.

317
00:22:44,705 --> 00:22:47,426
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's the virtue that calms down the animal.

318
00:22:47,854 --> 00:22:49,715
[SPEAKER_03]: The virtue calms down the mind.

319
00:22:50,495 --> 00:22:53,056
[SPEAKER_03]: Established in virtue leads to samādhi.

320
00:22:53,076 --> 00:22:55,476
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole relay chariots we did before.

321
00:22:58,757 --> 00:23:02,819
[SPEAKER_03]: So, exactly, not acting out of the unwholesome pressures calms the mind.

322
00:23:04,359 --> 00:23:10,401
[SPEAKER_03]: Until the recognition, discernment would then occur as a result of it.

323
00:23:10,981 --> 00:23:13,242
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, see, this is factually better, isn't it?

324
00:23:14,502 --> 00:23:15,523
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the mind would see it.

325
00:23:15,603 --> 00:23:16,243
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yes, it is.

326
00:23:17,131 --> 00:23:19,732
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you keep repeating that for as long as there are tendencies.

327
00:23:20,392 --> 00:23:21,773
[SPEAKER_03]: It comes up again and again.

328
00:23:21,913 --> 00:23:22,153
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

329
00:23:22,173 --> 00:23:22,773
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember this.

330
00:23:23,533 --> 00:23:34,057
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the delight is the gratification.

331
00:23:36,457 --> 00:23:39,899
[SPEAKER_03]: The danger... What's the danger?

332
00:23:41,819 --> 00:23:43,560
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the most immediate danger.

333
00:23:45,020 --> 00:23:46,761
[SPEAKER_03]: The suttas talk about it in different ways.

334
00:23:47,322 --> 00:23:48,924
[SPEAKER_03]: Depending, obviously, I think, on the audience.

335
00:23:49,164 --> 00:23:59,597
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometimes the danger is that, well, if you want sensuality, you have to go and plow fields and endure elements and work, work hard and endure lots of discomfort.

336
00:24:00,538 --> 00:24:04,583
[SPEAKER_03]: But what's the more immediate, what's the most immediate danger?

337
00:24:08,885 --> 00:24:16,970
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, that's obviously true, but... Also delight.

338
00:24:18,290 --> 00:24:24,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But from the point of view of, like, that... the liability to just being under that pressure.

339
00:24:24,534 --> 00:24:26,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Because delight is already pressuring.

340
00:24:26,735 --> 00:24:31,578
[SPEAKER_03]: That's... Well, yeah, the delight is pressuring, but he... you can handle the pressure.

341
00:24:31,798 --> 00:24:33,359
[SPEAKER_03]: Most people can, to some extent.

342
00:24:36,462 --> 00:24:43,045
[SPEAKER_03]: But what's the actual, you know, the danger, the chopping block, the death, all the similes that Buddha gave when he came to sensuality?

343
00:24:43,485 --> 00:24:44,866
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a bit more than just the pressure.

344
00:24:52,429 --> 00:24:56,010
[SPEAKER_03]: Pressure is the gateway for the communication and everything else.

345
00:24:56,951 --> 00:24:58,051
[SPEAKER_03]: Contact is the gateway.

346
00:24:58,291 --> 00:25:04,294
[SPEAKER_03]: But what is that danger that you want to convey to the mind?

347
00:25:07,338 --> 00:25:10,099
[SPEAKER_03]: That you can see for itself, then, and stop doing it.

348
00:25:12,239 --> 00:25:16,861
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the delight comes from the animal, does the danger come from the animal as well?

349
00:25:21,842 --> 00:25:29,945
[SPEAKER_02]: The completely wild, untamed thing that you are attached to, but you have no control over, that is your puppet.

350
00:25:29,965 --> 00:25:30,885
[SPEAKER_03]: But more specifically,

351
00:25:31,693 --> 00:25:32,713
[SPEAKER_03]: How does that manifest?

352
00:25:32,813 --> 00:25:35,174
[SPEAKER_03]: How does that wild, untamed nature would manifest?

353
00:25:36,114 --> 00:25:41,896
[SPEAKER_03]: So that you realize, oh, that is actually the petal, the heart of it.

354
00:25:50,699 --> 00:25:55,241
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you scale that child to its core, so it never does that ever again?

355
00:25:59,652 --> 00:26:07,558
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of its capacity to the right is the extent of its capacity to bite itself, to bite you.

356
00:26:08,639 --> 00:26:10,000
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

357
00:26:10,140 --> 00:26:11,962
[SPEAKER_03]: It's...

358
00:26:11,982 --> 00:26:19,367
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I said before, the immediate danger is liability.

359
00:26:21,409 --> 00:26:26,313
[SPEAKER_03]: You being liable to that animal just to turn against you at any given second.

360
00:26:28,768 --> 00:26:30,468
[SPEAKER_03]: But, what's in it for the animal?

361
00:26:31,268 --> 00:26:32,169
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the danger.

362
00:26:32,629 --> 00:26:38,410
[SPEAKER_03]: It's apparent to you, but you want also animals to see that as unbeneficial for itself.

363
00:26:42,851 --> 00:26:49,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's, you know, pleasant, unpleasant, neutral, whatever kind of suffering is there, it's because of craving.

364
00:26:49,692 --> 00:26:51,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of the... and that's it.

365
00:26:52,212 --> 00:26:55,853
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, when it's... when there's sensual craving,

366
00:26:58,581 --> 00:27:00,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yes, I see where you're getting at.

367
00:27:00,922 --> 00:27:02,603
[SPEAKER_03]: The craving is bad, fundamentally.

368
00:27:02,863 --> 00:27:03,963
[SPEAKER_03]: You're better off without it.

369
00:27:05,184 --> 00:27:15,547
[SPEAKER_03]: But the gratification, the danger and the escape are specifically need to be seen in the specific contexts of sensuality, of anything you depend on.

370
00:27:17,008 --> 00:27:20,889
[SPEAKER_03]: So, these are the specific messages that need to be conveyed to the mind.

371
00:27:22,410 --> 00:27:23,370
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, craving is bad.

372
00:27:24,121 --> 00:27:27,143
[SPEAKER_03]: It would be much like... everybody would be better off without it.

373
00:27:27,904 --> 00:27:29,205
[SPEAKER_03]: But the mind doesn't understand that.

374
00:27:29,645 --> 00:27:38,713
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you show it the delight that it comes from it, that is gratuitous, and it will stop delighting if you show it the danger of its own delight.

375
00:27:40,654 --> 00:27:46,379
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the danger for you, obviously, your interest there is to tame the animal, so it doesn't bite your face off.

376
00:27:47,600 --> 00:27:48,841
[SPEAKER_03]: But what's in it for the animal?

377
00:27:49,601 --> 00:27:52,964
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is it beneficial for the animal to not just bite your face off?

378
00:27:59,865 --> 00:28:01,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Its wildness is factually unpleasant.

379
00:28:01,506 --> 00:28:02,967
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm trying to say.

380
00:28:03,027 --> 00:28:08,891
[SPEAKER_03]: It's factually... Well, it's factually pleasant, but anyone doesn't see the facts, because that's the point.

381
00:28:09,191 --> 00:28:11,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you're wild, you don't see that as a problem.

382
00:28:13,874 --> 00:28:16,596
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to scare it to see it as a problem.

383
00:28:16,776 --> 00:28:18,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just slightly indirect.

384
00:28:18,857 --> 00:28:19,678
[SPEAKER_03]: Everything is indirect.

385
00:28:19,698 --> 00:28:25,081
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no... Or, like, less immediate than that also, that you're talking about.

386
00:28:26,363 --> 00:28:27,263
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no end.

387
00:28:27,384 --> 00:28:30,085
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, this is how I would describe it.

388
00:28:30,125 --> 00:28:34,487
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, in that direction, you just... there's no hope.

389
00:28:36,008 --> 00:28:38,469
[SPEAKER_03]: Again, like, imagine a crazed drug addict.

390
00:28:38,589 --> 00:28:39,009
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

391
00:28:39,049 --> 00:28:40,110
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't have a reason.

392
00:28:40,470 --> 00:28:41,290
[SPEAKER_03]: I know it's unpleasant.

393
00:28:41,310 --> 00:28:41,751
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't care.

394
00:28:41,791 --> 00:28:42,571
[SPEAKER_03]: I will still kill you.

395
00:28:42,771 --> 00:28:42,991
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

396
00:28:43,071 --> 00:28:44,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're in my way.

397
00:28:44,092 --> 00:28:44,512
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

398
00:28:45,392 --> 00:28:54,517
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you then, a person who is not reasonable, an animal that follows no reason or facts, how do you scare it to stop doing it?

399
00:29:05,930 --> 00:29:09,953
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my point.

400
00:29:09,994 --> 00:29:13,116
[SPEAKER_03]: Whenever there is a delight, there is no reason to that extent.

401
00:29:14,257 --> 00:29:21,963
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're presenting the the right contexts, but fundamentally, in order to get through, the danger becomes apparent.

402
00:29:22,164 --> 00:29:27,168
[SPEAKER_03]: It needs to sink in through the animal as well, that it's dangerous.

403
00:29:28,625 --> 00:29:34,029
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'm asking, for you it's going to be, oh, this thing, at any given second, can turn against me.

404
00:29:34,769 --> 00:29:36,271
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the most immediate danger.

405
00:29:37,591 --> 00:29:47,278
[SPEAKER_03]: You're presenting the context of, like, disagreeability, disgust, in things that he wants, so he loses the desire.

406
00:29:47,318 --> 00:29:56,185
[SPEAKER_03]: But, fundamentally, the escape comes from really recognizing the danger of even the slightest giving in to that... the slightest wanting of the animal.

407
00:29:57,258 --> 00:29:58,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm asking.

408
00:29:58,918 --> 00:30:01,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're asking about the danger not of giving in to it.

409
00:30:01,559 --> 00:30:04,020
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already, like, kind of secondary.

410
00:30:04,420 --> 00:30:05,660
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is not about...

411
00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:06,460
[SPEAKER_03]: The danger of delight.

412
00:30:06,540 --> 00:30:07,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the danger of delighting.

413
00:30:07,961 --> 00:30:09,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

414
00:30:09,381 --> 00:30:12,802
[SPEAKER_03]: The danger, gratification, danger and escape.

415
00:30:13,622 --> 00:30:16,143
[SPEAKER_03]: You recognize that gratification is the delight.

416
00:30:17,003 --> 00:30:18,243
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the danger of it?

417
00:30:19,484 --> 00:30:19,784
[SPEAKER_03]: Well...

418
00:30:22,804 --> 00:30:23,425
[SPEAKER_01]: The non-escape.

419
00:30:24,645 --> 00:30:25,145
[SPEAKER_01]: The non-escape.

420
00:30:26,563 --> 00:30:27,303
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more than that.

421
00:30:27,803 --> 00:30:28,944
[SPEAKER_02]: What you're describing, isn't it?

422
00:30:29,104 --> 00:30:33,806
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you would be in the context of relative calm, in the sense that you're not acting out.

423
00:30:33,846 --> 00:30:37,707
[SPEAKER_02]: But you've already done the... you've already put the boundaries around.

424
00:30:38,387 --> 00:30:38,967
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all done.

425
00:30:39,147 --> 00:30:42,188
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's already in a state of relative... Of course.

426
00:30:42,429 --> 00:30:46,050
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, so, so... And it's faint, because you wouldn't be seeing the animal to begin with.

427
00:30:46,170 --> 00:30:47,150
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's all covered.

428
00:30:47,170 --> 00:30:49,011
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't need to rehash... Because you're delighting in death.

429
00:30:49,171 --> 00:30:50,691
[SPEAKER_00]: What is subject to... what is death?

430
00:30:50,991 --> 00:30:53,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're... to the extent of the delight, you're...

431
00:30:55,896 --> 00:30:59,417
[SPEAKER_03]: To the extent of the light... That's the extent of death.

432
00:31:00,057 --> 00:31:01,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Of basically... What you're being subject to death.

433
00:31:01,658 --> 00:31:02,438
[SPEAKER_03]: Self-destruction.

434
00:31:02,458 --> 00:31:03,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-destruction, yeah.

435
00:31:03,739 --> 00:31:06,680
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the message you want to correct... convey to it.

436
00:31:06,800 --> 00:31:08,180
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's the message you get from it.

437
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:09,761
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

438
00:31:10,661 --> 00:31:23,405
[SPEAKER_03]: When you recognize that the animal can attack you at any given time, you realize... like, at any given time, regardless of the circumstances, regardless... this thing from the inside can just end me.

439
00:31:25,437 --> 00:31:28,418
[SPEAKER_03]: And you realize, oh, there is nothing in the world that can come in between that.

440
00:31:30,038 --> 00:31:32,398
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's obviously, this would be the scariest feeling.

441
00:31:33,118 --> 00:31:39,479
[SPEAKER_03]: If you experience it on account of virtue and sense restraint, which you probably will at some point, you can contain it.

442
00:31:39,739 --> 00:31:44,100
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you realize, well, as long as I don't act out of this, it's contained.

443
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:48,861
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you would have to see at least some extent of it.

444
00:31:48,941 --> 00:31:50,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, that is the danger.

445
00:31:51,561 --> 00:31:53,942
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's not the danger because you're paired with the mind.

446
00:31:55,141 --> 00:31:57,263
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not gonna kill you just because you're paired with it.

447
00:31:57,883 --> 00:32:00,545
[SPEAKER_03]: It will kill you because it's wild while you're paired with it.

448
00:32:02,447 --> 00:32:16,397
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you realize any trace of the light that I would tolerate or allow or not address is the direct link to this murderous thing that's inside of me.

449
00:32:18,899 --> 00:32:20,660
[SPEAKER_03]: Like that there's nothing that can come in between.

450
00:32:21,461 --> 00:32:22,422
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you realize, well,

451
00:32:23,352 --> 00:32:25,833
[SPEAKER_03]: That, in and of itself, is the end of the animal.

452
00:32:26,673 --> 00:32:29,375
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it will act for its own harm, if it does that.

453
00:32:32,576 --> 00:32:39,839
[SPEAKER_03]: So, animal delighting, in sensuality, means animal delighting in its own absolute demise.

454
00:32:40,399 --> 00:32:41,660
[SPEAKER_03]: And it doesn't want to end.

455
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:42,860
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would it?

456
00:32:44,501 --> 00:32:46,602
[SPEAKER_03]: But you need to make that connection for it.

457
00:32:48,624 --> 00:32:50,004
[SPEAKER_03]: Delight means the demise.

458
00:32:50,044 --> 00:32:56,186
[SPEAKER_03]: Not, oh, if I give in to this and then this and then this, then the danger of sensuality comes and becomes apparent.

459
00:32:56,206 --> 00:32:56,566
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

460
00:32:57,206 --> 00:33:00,087
[SPEAKER_03]: You're already acting out of the pressure of sensual delight.

461
00:33:00,867 --> 00:33:01,948
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not seeing the animal.

462
00:33:01,968 --> 00:33:04,829
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not addressing the delight that comes from the animal.

463
00:33:07,609 --> 00:33:13,531
[SPEAKER_03]: The hidden murderer with that unpredictable intent, it's there because of that.

464
00:33:14,651 --> 00:33:16,872
[SPEAKER_03]: You remove the delight, you're the best friends.

465
00:33:20,102 --> 00:33:21,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing can cause you to suffer ever again.

466
00:33:25,785 --> 00:33:26,585
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you remove the light?

467
00:33:26,625 --> 00:33:39,192
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, as we've just been describing, you keep presenting this context to it, and whenever you even recognize, so you have to keep a watchful eye on the space in between, quote-unquote.

468
00:33:40,513 --> 00:33:48,757
[SPEAKER_03]: So, by putting back the choice, by making the choice clear to the animal, that's how you can stop doing it.

469
00:33:51,551 --> 00:33:53,152
[SPEAKER_03]: By connecting, by associating.

470
00:33:53,332 --> 00:33:57,133
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, choice of delight means choice of this, and it's like, okay, thank you.

471
00:34:01,514 --> 00:34:07,016
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it's like, initially, it's like the scariest thing, but there is a liberation hiding in it.

472
00:34:09,037 --> 00:34:14,159
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's like, oh, the murderous, intimate friend, how the suttas say.

473
00:34:15,419 --> 00:34:17,740
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, but only because of the delight.

474
00:34:18,860 --> 00:34:20,121
[SPEAKER_03]: Only because of the craving.

475
00:34:20,866 --> 00:34:23,307
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because of its own faticity or something.

476
00:34:24,448 --> 00:34:35,713
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if I convey the message that that intent, that that craving and delight is killing itself as well, it will stop doing it and I'll be free as a result of it.

477
00:34:36,334 --> 00:34:44,558
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what I was saying, I think, in that talk.

478
00:34:45,218 --> 00:34:50,501
[SPEAKER_03]: The danger is that the mind turns against itself in that fit.

479
00:34:53,117 --> 00:35:00,323
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I said, you can be living with a wild dog, but you're constantly feeding it treats and constantly occupy its attention with activities.

480
00:35:00,443 --> 00:35:02,024
[SPEAKER_03]: It won't turn and look at you, in a sense of like,

481
00:35:22,588 --> 00:35:23,328
[SPEAKER_03]: You're a stranger.

482
00:35:23,348 --> 00:35:24,549
[SPEAKER_03]: What are you doing in my territory?

483
00:35:24,589 --> 00:35:25,369
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't want you here.

484
00:35:26,690 --> 00:35:31,091
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you start saying no to your distractions and stuff, that's the first thing you... it's going to become apparent.

485
00:35:32,032 --> 00:35:36,953
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I said before, like, people start panicking just because, you know, they stop watching TV and listening to the news.

486
00:35:37,814 --> 00:35:39,234
[SPEAKER_03]: There are panic attacks and whatnot.

487
00:35:40,635 --> 00:35:42,736
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're just being dependent on constant activity.

488
00:35:43,176 --> 00:35:51,439
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's also, like, deep down, anybody, everybody knows, like, people that kind of... and I think most people depend on some sort of activities, to some extent.

489
00:35:52,480 --> 00:35:59,062
[SPEAKER_03]: And deep down, they know that if they were to stop, things would get bad, internally.

490
00:36:00,483 --> 00:36:03,403
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, they just go until they basically crash.

491
00:36:04,464 --> 00:36:05,124
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no stopping.

492
00:36:05,324 --> 00:36:09,465
[SPEAKER_03]: They can't stop, because if they stop, then this animal just comes and stands in front of them.

493
00:36:11,226 --> 00:36:18,328
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like, oh... That's what Buddha meant when he said to Angulimala, well, I stopped.

494
00:36:19,388 --> 00:36:20,969
[SPEAKER_03]: You're the one still moving.

495
00:36:23,480 --> 00:36:27,501
[SPEAKER_03]: He stopped any activity on account of the pressure of the animal.

496
00:36:27,841 --> 00:36:29,142
[SPEAKER_03]: It just does not exist anymore.

497
00:36:29,182 --> 00:36:31,122
[SPEAKER_03]: The animal is tamed, so it's completely still.

498
00:36:33,763 --> 00:36:41,565
[SPEAKER_03]: And, interestingly enough, Angulimal understood it, despite all his other actions.

499
00:36:41,585 --> 00:36:48,567
[SPEAKER_03]: It means he was probably very acutely aware of that pressure, and that he was acting out of that very thing.

500
00:36:51,308 --> 00:36:52,748
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the light comes from the animal,

501
00:36:54,263 --> 00:36:55,403
[SPEAKER_03]: Danger comes from the animal.

502
00:36:57,384 --> 00:37:01,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Removing the delight frees you from the danger.

503
00:37:03,286 --> 00:37:05,807
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's basically the escape.

504
00:37:06,347 --> 00:37:15,910
[SPEAKER_03]: When it stops delighting, on its own accord, because it sees for itself that, oh, yeah, that would have been to my own... that would have led to my own harm.

505
00:37:18,151 --> 00:37:21,232
[SPEAKER_03]: That's even when, like, that's even freedom is out of your hands, so to speak.

506
00:37:22,273 --> 00:37:23,433
[SPEAKER_03]: Because now the animal's tamed.

507
00:37:25,087 --> 00:37:26,988
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to maintain it.

508
00:37:27,008 --> 00:37:27,809
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to do it.

509
00:37:28,710 --> 00:37:29,831
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to keep checking in.

510
00:37:30,631 --> 00:37:38,157
[SPEAKER_03]: You just saw for itself that these things are always unbeneficial, like, to the core.

511
00:37:39,358 --> 00:37:46,043
[SPEAKER_03]: And just an inkling towards the delight would bring that whole weight of terror implied in it.

512
00:37:53,248 --> 00:37:59,010
[SPEAKER_03]: So, again, it's not like you need to free yourself from delight, or you need to figure out the danger.

513
00:38:02,712 --> 00:38:03,832
[SPEAKER_03]: The animal needs to see that.

514
00:38:06,233 --> 00:38:12,876
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in a way, teaching that, it's less... less intense, let's put it that way.

515
00:38:14,317 --> 00:38:16,538
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you think you need to figure it out yourself,

516
00:38:17,963 --> 00:38:19,624
[SPEAKER_03]: And I'm not seeing it, and I'm not seeing it.

517
00:38:19,944 --> 00:38:30,689
[SPEAKER_03]: You'll be just... just going in circles, filling up with doubt and so on, and still not really arriving at any conclusion, because there is no conclusion to arrive to.

518
00:38:30,729 --> 00:38:31,729
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't pertain to you.

519
00:38:33,170 --> 00:38:34,090
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you know, no, no, no.

520
00:38:34,591 --> 00:38:46,676
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about not acting out, my body, speech and mind, established in virtue, sense restraint, non-distraction, and then just maintaining this context, not too much, not too little.

521
00:38:48,477 --> 00:38:50,158
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what animal sees.

522
00:38:51,880 --> 00:38:54,942
[SPEAKER_03]: It sees your little body language when you're not thinking about it.

523
00:38:55,442 --> 00:38:57,064
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, now you're always thinking about it.

524
00:38:57,944 --> 00:39:01,907
[SPEAKER_03]: So, your little body language is the intentional messages to the animal.

525
00:39:02,328 --> 00:39:04,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you learn, oh, that's the level it reads.

526
00:39:07,552 --> 00:39:11,255
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why even these contemplations don't need to be necessarily unpleasant.

527
00:39:12,616 --> 00:39:18,040
[SPEAKER_03]: It's only unpleasant if you've been giving in to the delight and then the danger of it

528
00:39:18,895 --> 00:39:22,276
[SPEAKER_03]: been made visible, well, then you feel the weight of that terror.

529
00:39:23,736 --> 00:39:32,518
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you don't give in to the delight, the implication is more than enough for you to see the full extent of it, without needing to be terrorized, in that sense.

530
00:39:33,018 --> 00:39:35,739
[SPEAKER_03]: Because no amount of animal's delight can make you act.

531
00:39:36,199 --> 00:39:37,159
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the whole point.

532
00:39:37,960 --> 00:39:39,240
[SPEAKER_03]: You are the one choosing to do so.

533
00:39:42,520 --> 00:39:52,984
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even if you're all filled with confusion and doubt on some days, and you don't see the animal, and like this, and everything is now, nothing's clear, well, what's clear is what not to do by body, speech and mind.

534
00:39:54,264 --> 00:39:58,626
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even if I'm not seeing the animal, I know it's close, because I'm not giving in to its pressure.

535
00:39:59,506 --> 00:39:59,886
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

